@page "/aboutsecurity"
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Authorization
@inject AuthenticationStateProvider AuthenticationStateProvider

<PageTitle>About Security</PageTitle>

<h1>About Security</h1>

<AuthorizeView>
    <Authorized>
        <p>This application enforces authentication and role-based authorization using ASP.NET Core Identity. Access is restricted to authorized users for sensitive operations, and roles such as Admin, Staff, and User are used to control permissions.</p>

        <h4>Key controls</h4>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Authentication</strong>: ASP.NET Core Identity with secure password policies, email confirmation, and optional passkeys. Password policy requires a mix of upper/lowercase, digits, and non-alphanumeric characters.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Authorization</strong>: Role-based policies (Admin, Staff, User) protect endpoints and UI actions. Critical operations require Admin or Staff roles and are enforced server-side in services and controllers.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Data protection</strong>: Application data protection keys can be persisted to a shared key ring (configured via DataProtection:KeyRingPath). This ensures data protection keys survive restarts and can be shared between instances.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Database migrations</strong>: On startup the application attempts to apply migrations (MigrateAsync) with retry logic. CI builds do not apply migrations automatically; deployments should run migrations as a controlled step.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>CI/CD and secrets</strong>: Deployment secrets (e.g., seed accounts) are provided via repository secrets and written into env files during deploy. Never commit secrets to the repository.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Container security</strong>: Docker images are built from minimal base images and scanned in pipeline. Container runtime uses environment-based configuration and does not store secrets in code.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Logging and monitoring</strong>: Startup logs include migration and seed activities. Configure external logging/monitoring (e.g., syslog, Azure Monitor) in production for auditability.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <p>If you need more detail about a specific control, contact an administrator or view the repository Docs folder for operational guidance.</p>
    </Authorized>
    <NotAuthorized>
        <div class="alert alert-warning">You are not authorized to view this page. Please <a href="/Account/Login">log in</a> with an account that has access.</div>
    </NotAuthorized>
</AuthorizeView>
